{"alfredo":{"id":"1b3a1528-a0ad-4c38-aeb5-96e37a5b8a4a","word":"Alfredo","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"sauce made from butter and Parmesan","tags":["masculine"],"word":"molho Alfredo"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["English given names","English male given names","English male given names from Italian"],"links":[["given name","given name"]],"glosses":["A male given name from Italian."],"id":"f4418d0b-0975-42c8-86aa-d64259649720"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"alfred":{"id":"87bd55e4-2c56-485d-b079-50defb3a5b98","word":"Alfred","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Male given name","word":"Alfredo"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["king","king"],["England","England"]],"related":[{"word":"Ælfred","tags":["alternative"]}],"raw_glosses":["(individuals) Alfred the Great, early king of England."],"qualifier":"individuals","glosses":["Alfred the Great, early king of England."],"id":"cae6dbef-fd8f-4fa3-b60c-fe9bdd1befe7"},{"examples":[{"text":"Unfortunately for me my father had combined diplomacy with a study of Anglo-Saxon history and, of course with my mother's consent, he gave me the name of Alfred, one of his heroes ( I believe she had boggled at Aelfred ). This Christian name, for some inexplicable reason, had become corrupted in the eyes of our middle-class world; it belonged exclusively now to the working class and was usually abbreviated to Alf. Perhaps that was why Doctor Fisher, the inventor of Dentophil Bouquet, never called me anything but Jones, even after I married his daughter.","ref":"1980, Graham Greene, Doctor Fisher of Geneva, or the Bomb Party (fiction), →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[154,160]],"id":"971f5d83-a5d9-49ff-92e1-076d99fc46c2"},{"text":"You give a kid a name like Cameron / or Alfred, or something like that, / and they end up wearing glasses / and looking at computers for the rest of their life.","ref":"1998, Steven Herrick,